WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.451
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:13.610
Happy Thursday,
everybody, and welcome

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:15.590
to the Data Science Hangout.

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:18.530
I hope everybody is
having a great week.

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:21.160
If we haven't had a chance
to meet yet, I'm Rachael.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.110
It's so nice to meet you.

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:26.470
If it is your first time
joining us at the Hangout,

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:30.590
let us know in the chat so we
can all welcome you in as well.

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:34.340
This is our open space to chat
about data-science leadership,

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:36.490
questions you're
facing, and getting

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:38.590
to hear about what's going
on in the world of data

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:40.610
across different industries.

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:43.840
So every week, we get to feature
a different data-science leader

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:46.900
as my co-host here to help
lead our discussion and answer

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:49.080
questions from you all.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.550
And so together, we're
all dedicated to making

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.570
this a welcoming
environment for everybody.

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:56.290
And I'd love to hear
from everyone, no matter

00:00:56.290 --> 00:01:00.300
your level of experience or
area of work or background.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.760
It's also totally OK to
just listen in if you want.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:06.660
There's also three ways to
ask questions and provide

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.750
your own perspective
on certain topics, too.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:14.370
So you can jump in by
raising your hand on Zoom.

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:17.470
You could put questions
into the Zoom chat.

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:19.590
And feel free to just put
a little star next to it

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:21.300
if you're in a coffee
shop or something,

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:24.570
or your dog is barking, and
you want me to read it instead.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:29.220
We also have a Slido link, which
my colleagues will share here

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:31.860
in the chat in just a
second, where you can also

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.470
ask questions anonymously.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.860
We do share the
recordings of each session

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:40.320
up to the Posit YouTube
and the Hangout site.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:45.030
So you can always go back and
rewatch or share with a friend.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:47.550
But with all that, thank
you all for joining us again

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:48.210
this Thursday.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:51.720
And thank you so much, Art,
for joining us as the co-host.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:56.160
Art is a private investor
and former chairman, CEO,

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.980
and president of
OppenheimerFunds.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:00.780
And Art, I'd love
to kick things off

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.030
with having you
introduce yourself,

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.750
maybe sharing a bit about your
journey, and also something

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:08.520
you like to do
outside of work, too.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:09.630
Sure, sure.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:13.980
Well, thanks very
much for inviting me.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:20.100
I'm speaking to you all from
New York City, where we have yet

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:20.950
to see winter start.

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:22.950
Supposedly, we might get
some snow this weekend,

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:25.960
but we haven't had any
snow at all this winter.

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:26.760
So that's strange.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.370
I'm sitting here in my
study, and I'm drinking out

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:32.383
of my ggplot mug, which--

00:02:32.383 --> 00:02:33.300
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Nice.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.730
ART STEINMETZ: --was designed
by [? Selena ?] Carter.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:40.340
She graciously let me
put this design on--

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:42.200
what is one of those
sites that lets

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.580
you print out your own
T-shirts and stuff like that.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:47.980
So I had myself a coffee
mug made with that.

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:52.360
Anyway, so I'm
technically retired.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:54.250
I used to work on Wall Street.

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:57.370
I was a money manager
for most of my time,

00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:02.170
before I went into a leadership
position at OppenheimerFunds.

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:05.230
And then I stepped down when
we sold the company to Invesco

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:08.620
back in 2019.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.830
Since then, I've been involved
in a lot of this and that,

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:13.930
board work, philanthropic work.

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:20.650
But what I love most is being
a data-science hobbyist.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:26.980
So I started working with R,
the language, back in 2006,

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:28.960
I think it was.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:30.410
And it just grew from there.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:35.230
And I've just really enjoyed
being able to use it first

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:39.940
in my professional work, and
then now more as a hobbyist,

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:44.140
and, as part of
that, really enjoyed

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:51.190
becoming part of the
R community and really

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:57.100
appreciating the joys
of open-source software

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:01.870
and its ability to put
power into people's hands

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:07.690
to really get good work
done to do good analysis.

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:13.150
Like anything else,
you can do lousy work.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:16.329
[LAUGHS] And it's--

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:18.970
as we've seen with
ChatGPT, which

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:23.140
speaks very authoritatively and
confidently but sometimes spews

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:26.830
nonsense, it's certainly
dangerous to put

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:29.020
beautiful charts and
graphs into people's hands

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:31.490
that tell the wrong story.

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:33.340
And I think that's an
important narrative

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:39.060
around how open-source
propagates around

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:40.020
in organizations.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:41.340
That's a cautionary lesson.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:45.010
But anyway, we can talk
a little bit about that.

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:47.370
So anyway, that's what I do now.

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:55.530
You can see my blog at
ArtSteinmetz.com, where I share

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:58.260
my data-science experiments.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:00.930
And I have to stress, I'm not
a data-science professional,

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:01.430
right?

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:05.010
I'm an investment professional.

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:09.450
So I am very much an
amateur, with all the caveats

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:11.160
that that implies.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:15.240
But again, I really
enjoy hanging out

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:17.580
with smart people
like you folks,

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.220
who are in the
main-- actual data

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:26.200
scientists or actual
practitioners, professionally.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:31.860
So Rachael said, so what do
you do for fun or something

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:32.820
outside of work?

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:35.430
Well, again, since
I'm retired, I

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:38.250
spend a lot of my time
playing around with R

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:39.640
while I'm sitting at my desk.

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:42.120
I like to ski.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:44.830
I like to hike.

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:49.530
I was a home brewer.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:52.230
I retired from that when
I moved from New Jersey

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:55.410
back into New York City.

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:59.760
But I can talk at length
about IPA recipes,

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:03.570
if anybody wants to do that.

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:07.140
But again, one of
the things that

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:09.690
is nice about playing around
with open-source software

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:12.110
is, it's not an expensive hobby.

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:17.910
Guys are famous for spending
enormous amounts of money

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:22.110
to buy gear for their hobbies,
whether it's fishing or cars

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:24.510
or whatever.

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:28.050
Fortunately, open-source
software is--

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.630
whatever I can come up
with, build in my head,

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:32.680
is what I'm spending money on.

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:37.110
So anyway, that's
enough about me.

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:37.800
I love it.

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.260
[CHUCKLES] I know the
mountains are getting a lot

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:42.270
of snow hopefully this weekend.

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:44.130
Are you going to
head out skiing?

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:46.710
Yeah, well, I was
in Big Sky, Montana

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:48.210
at the beginning of January.

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:52.140
And at 64 years old,
I probably shouldn't

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:53.880
do the double black diamonds.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:56.670
But I went down one
that had thin cover.

00:06:56.670 --> 00:07:02.430
And I hooked my armpit around a
tree stump, and I tore my pec.

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:05.140
So I had orthopedic
surgery a couple weeks ago.

00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:07.470
So I've got to keep--

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:10.270
stay in a sling for
another few weeks.

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:13.980
But hopefully I'm going to get
a little late-season skiing

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:16.980
in this year when I go back
out, and hopefully won't do it

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:17.790
again--

00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:18.870
tear myself up again.

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:20.570
Oh, wow.

00:07:20.570 --> 00:07:24.020
Well, I hope you're
feeling OK now.

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:26.690
So I know, when we were talking
yesterday a little bit, Art,

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:30.020
you were mentioning, your
journey into data science

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:32.660
started when you became
a little bit frustrated

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:34.520
by the limits of Excel.

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:38.780
And I'm just curious to hear
about how that journey first

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:41.180
started and any tips
you have for people

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:43.460
who are also trying
to help others

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:46.430
in their organization
who'd love to learn, too.

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:51.080
Yeah, and the
disclaimer here is partly,

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:54.920
say, it was a
different time then.

00:07:54.920 --> 00:08:04.550
A lot of times, when we
talk about cultural issues,

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:07.220
we talk about, well, it
was different back then.

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:10.940
And it was different back
then for data science

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:14.930
because back then,
employees at my firm

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:19.670
couldn't install any software
they pleased on their computer.

00:08:19.670 --> 00:08:22.580
And that has--
certainly not true

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:26.250
in any responsible
organization today.

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:27.560
But it was a different time.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:31.490
So even though I could
have put in the budget

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:38.090
request for Matlab, I'm
just inherently cheap.

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:41.000
And so when I learned about R,
I was really interested in that.

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:44.630
But I had become frustrated
with the limitations of Excel.

00:08:44.630 --> 00:08:50.210
Playing around with-- like
spreadsheet geeks will do,

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:52.130
I had these giant
spreadsheets, and they

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:54.680
had a lot of VBA
macros, Visual Basic

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:56.180
macros, involved with them.

00:08:56.180 --> 00:09:01.070
But they were increasingly
difficult to maintain.

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:06.080
And I would go back
and pull up a project

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:08.780
I had worked on
several months earlier

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:12.830
and was not able to figure
out what the heck I was doing

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:15.320
or what I was trying to
accomplish in that thing.

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:18.680
Or if I said, well, oh,
I can use my former work

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:20.840
because now this new
project I'm working on

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:24.290
is kind of like that, so let
me reuse some of my old work,

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:26.450
figuring out what
I did back then--

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:30.960
what former me had done-- just
was a huge pain in the neck.

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:33.590
So I was frustrated by that.

00:09:33.590 --> 00:09:35.870
That was probably my
biggest frustration.

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:43.370
And in addition, just
the power of Excel,

00:09:43.370 --> 00:09:50.990
when working on large data
sets with lots of computations,

00:09:50.990 --> 00:09:53.510
was also not--

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:55.985
I was running up against
the limitations there.

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:00.900
But again, this was just me.

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:03.290
There was nothing I would
say I was in production

00:10:03.290 --> 00:10:04.610
for other people to use.

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:06.988
I had dashboards with
graphs and stuff,

00:10:06.988 --> 00:10:08.780
and I would share those
around in meetings.

00:10:08.780 --> 00:10:10.580
But it was really just me.

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:13.040
So it wasn't really an
enterprise application.

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:16.910
But anyway, so I
discovered R and was

00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:24.170
hooked immediately and was able
to do some really cool work.

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:27.300
But I was an island
in the organization.

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:32.780
And I think many of us who
use open-source software may

00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:35.750
feel that way.

00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:39.260
But slowly but
surely, other people

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:41.930
started asking me, well,
how did you do that?

00:10:41.930 --> 00:10:44.600
Or that doesn't look like
an Excel chart or something

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:46.970
like that.

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:49.870
And at the same
time, my organization

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:56.830
started becoming, in
general, more quantitative.

00:10:56.830 --> 00:10:59.410
Oppenheimer was-- and now,
it is part of Invesco--

00:10:59.410 --> 00:11:02.890
is a money-management firm.

00:11:02.890 --> 00:11:09.520
We manage portfolios of stocks
and bonds and whatever else.

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:14.710
But we were very much an
old-school, fundamental

00:11:14.710 --> 00:11:17.480
analysis, which is great.

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:19.930
It just doesn't come at it
from the quantitative side.

00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:23.620
It's a much more
touchy-feely type of thing.

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:26.590
And like the rest
of the industry,

00:11:26.590 --> 00:11:33.250
we all started becoming
more interested in applying

00:11:33.250 --> 00:11:36.850
computational power to huge
amounts of information,

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:39.760
because the price of
computation is going down.

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:42.910
The accessibility of huge
information was going up.

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:45.580
So it just was part
of a natural trend.

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:54.090
And I was more fluent
than the average portfolio

00:11:54.090 --> 00:11:57.570
manager in my firm with
the techniques needed

00:11:57.570 --> 00:12:01.530
to work with that data, because
of just my freelance work

00:12:01.530 --> 00:12:05.190
with R. So I think
that helped me out.

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:10.290
That did not play a part
in my career advancement.

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:12.430
I don't want to
suggest that at all.

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:16.410
But I also happened
to advance further

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:19.950
in my career at
Oppenheimer, ultimately

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:26.010
becoming CEO for the last
five years of my stay there.

00:12:26.010 --> 00:12:30.540
But I continued to
play around with R,

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:34.170
even though, I have to
admit, I was playing hooky

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:39.720
from my real job when I was
doing data-science work.

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:42.000
I would do analysis
of our funds, analysis

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:45.750
of our competitors' funds on
various types of performance

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:47.050
metrics and so forth.

00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:49.350
But as CEO, that was not my job.

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:52.200
There were people I could
ask to do that work for me.

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:58.050
But that was a release
for me and a little bit

00:12:58.050 --> 00:13:00.030
of an unwind for doing that.

00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:03.330
But at the same time,
people in my organization

00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:10.530
understood that I was very
sympathetic or receptive

00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:13.620
to using these kinds of
tools in the organization.

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:18.690
And I was constantly
evangelizing about

00:13:18.690 --> 00:13:22.620
or thinking about ways that
our organization could apply

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:28.140
more quantitative techniques
to upping our game

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:30.550
all across the business.

00:13:30.550 --> 00:13:34.980
So people-- there
were other islands

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:38.140
around our company of people who
were using this kind of stuff,

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:41.940
whether it was R or
Python or other tools.

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:46.050
And they knew that I
was very much a champion

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:47.500
of those sorts of things.

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:50.250
What that allowed us to
do, from their standpoint,

00:13:50.250 --> 00:13:55.410
practically-- it allowed
them to fast track permission

00:13:55.410 --> 00:13:58.410
to use these tools or to get
software updates or things

00:13:58.410 --> 00:14:02.750
like that, because as I
said, we all got to the point

00:14:02.750 --> 00:14:05.210
where you couldn't just
install whatever the heck

00:14:05.210 --> 00:14:06.590
you wanted on your computer.

00:14:06.590 --> 00:14:09.110
So at least they
knew that the IT guys

00:14:09.110 --> 00:14:13.560
were going to be sympathetic
to their requests.

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:16.275
And this is a dichotomy
I'm sure many of you

00:14:16.275 --> 00:14:17.900
see in your organizations
all the time.

00:14:17.900 --> 00:14:22.610
There's an inherent tension
between the high priests in IT

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:24.890
and the individuals
on their desks

00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:30.230
who want to be free to use
these open-source tools.

00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:34.970
And having leadership in
the organization understand

00:14:34.970 --> 00:14:37.650
and sympathizing with
that is very important.

00:14:37.650 --> 00:14:40.770
One of the great things
about Posit, of course,

00:14:40.770 --> 00:14:51.750
is that Posit has evolved
to really facilitate

00:14:51.750 --> 00:14:53.620
easing of that
tension, if you will,

00:14:53.620 --> 00:14:57.480
by providing walled-garden
types of tools where

00:14:57.480 --> 00:15:01.890
there can be auditing and
control of the versioning

00:15:01.890 --> 00:15:04.088
of all the various
packages and languages

00:15:04.088 --> 00:15:06.130
and so forth that people
have on their computers,

00:15:06.130 --> 00:15:10.050
but also letting people
have access to those tools

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:15.810
and not be constrained
by a large bureaucracy

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:17.980
for every little thing
they want to use.

00:15:17.980 --> 00:15:23.100
So I think Posit is doing
great work on reducing friction

00:15:23.100 --> 00:15:24.610
in the enterprise.

00:15:24.610 --> 00:15:25.950
So I like that.

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:27.182
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:27.182 --> 00:15:29.640
ART STEINMETZ: So again, we
started using these tools more.

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:31.730
But here's the
other ironic thing.

00:15:31.730 --> 00:15:33.580
So I'm an investment person.

00:15:33.580 --> 00:15:37.080
And like I said, I would like
to use quantitative tools

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:41.280
to look at time series of
interest rates and stock prices

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:48.210
and correlations and
capital asset pricing model

00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:51.340
metrics around investments
and stuff like that.

00:15:51.340 --> 00:15:55.730
And while I was an
evangelist for this stuff,

00:15:55.730 --> 00:15:59.120
the people that were getting
more excited about this

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:02.630
were not my fellow portfolio
managers and investment

00:16:02.630 --> 00:16:03.260
analysts.

00:16:03.260 --> 00:16:05.660
It was the guys in sales,
the people in sales

00:16:05.660 --> 00:16:08.960
that were getting more
interested in this,

00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:14.440
because like the
investment people,

00:16:14.440 --> 00:16:19.990
they were getting
terabytes of data

00:16:19.990 --> 00:16:23.530
from their
organizations, stuff that

00:16:23.530 --> 00:16:25.090
would come through our CRM--

00:16:25.090 --> 00:16:28.690
we used Salesforce--
or the advisors

00:16:28.690 --> 00:16:29.960
who would sell our funds.

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:32.140
So we were the asset managers.

00:16:32.140 --> 00:16:36.580
And then we would have
advisors or stockbrokers--

00:16:36.580 --> 00:16:40.990
financial advisors-- who would
sell it to retail individuals.

00:16:40.990 --> 00:16:47.780
And they, for a fee, would let
us see their customer data--

00:16:47.780 --> 00:16:50.320
what people were buying,
what people selling,

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:53.260
whether they were buying more
growth stocks or value stocks

00:16:53.260 --> 00:16:54.370
or whatever it is.

00:16:54.370 --> 00:16:56.800
And our salespeople would find
those tools very valuable.

00:16:56.800 --> 00:16:58.300
But it was a data dump.

00:16:58.300 --> 00:17:01.060
It was a deluge coming
from different sources.

00:17:01.060 --> 00:17:05.230
A lot of places, it
was unstructured data.

00:17:05.230 --> 00:17:09.040
And even when it was structured,
the formats from Morgan Stanley

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:12.490
were different than the
formats from Merrill Lynch

00:17:12.490 --> 00:17:14.290
and this kind of stuff.

00:17:14.290 --> 00:17:19.750
But our salespeople-- they got
it sooner than our investment

00:17:19.750 --> 00:17:24.579
people, that we can use
open-source data-science tools

00:17:24.579 --> 00:17:26.500
to make some sense of this.

00:17:29.350 --> 00:17:31.660
And even today,
they're the ones that

00:17:31.660 --> 00:17:34.030
are-- at Invesco,
my former colleagues

00:17:34.030 --> 00:17:36.550
at Oppenheimer, now at
Invesco-- are the ones pushing

00:17:36.550 --> 00:17:41.410
the envelope on this, on the
distribution organization.

00:17:41.410 --> 00:17:44.290
One of the things
that is a crazy thing

00:17:44.290 --> 00:17:50.043
about the mutual-fund business
is, people buy mutual funds,

00:17:50.043 --> 00:17:51.460
and then they
decide to sell them.

00:17:51.460 --> 00:17:53.168
Maybe they need to
sell them because they

00:17:53.168 --> 00:17:54.760
need the money for
other purposes,

00:17:54.760 --> 00:17:58.420
someone dies and the money
gets distributed in the estate,

00:17:58.420 --> 00:18:02.150
or the performance stinks and
people stop liking the fund,

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:03.550
and so they say they want out.

00:18:03.550 --> 00:18:06.730
All of those things
trigger redemptions.

00:18:06.730 --> 00:18:13.300
Now, the industry, overall, has
a background redemption rate

00:18:13.300 --> 00:18:15.070
of about 25% a year.

00:18:15.070 --> 00:18:18.520
That means, just to stay even
with how much money we manage,

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:25.390
we have to go out and resell
25% of our total asset base

00:18:25.390 --> 00:18:26.380
every year.

00:18:26.380 --> 00:18:28.390
So that's billions and
billions of dollars.

00:18:28.390 --> 00:18:30.643
And the other crazy thing--

00:18:30.643 --> 00:18:31.810
well, it's not crazy at all.

00:18:31.810 --> 00:18:35.360
But we sell-- we pay
commissions to sales people.

00:18:35.360 --> 00:18:38.740
So getting a dollar
is much more expensive

00:18:38.740 --> 00:18:40.450
than keeping a dollar.

00:18:40.450 --> 00:18:44.230
So we could drop a lot of
money right to the bottom line

00:18:44.230 --> 00:18:46.300
if we could cut our
redemption rate.

00:18:46.300 --> 00:18:48.880
So I sat down with
our salespeople.

00:18:48.880 --> 00:18:55.120
That became a thing I was
really pounding the table about.

00:18:55.120 --> 00:18:57.970
So they went, and
we put together

00:18:57.970 --> 00:19:05.200
a data-science team of about
nine PhD data-analytics people.

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:08.140
And this was all coming
out of the sales budget.

00:19:08.140 --> 00:19:11.770
They cheerfully
paid for all this.

00:19:11.770 --> 00:19:14.170
And we started
looking at, how can we

00:19:14.170 --> 00:19:15.405
reduce our redemption rate?

00:19:15.405 --> 00:19:16.780
At that time, our
redemption rate

00:19:16.780 --> 00:19:19.690
was running above the
industry average of about 27.

00:19:19.690 --> 00:19:21.700
And the nice thing when
you're a big firm--

00:19:21.700 --> 00:19:23.200
you can run A/B tests.

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:26.080
We tried different
kinds of interventions,

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:28.750
different kind of
marketing materials,

00:19:28.750 --> 00:19:35.740
changed who we visited and how
much time we spent with people,

00:19:35.740 --> 00:19:37.600
tried so many different things.

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:40.430
And we found out what
worked and what didn't work.

00:19:40.430 --> 00:19:44.890
And if people want some of the
details, I can go into that.

00:19:44.890 --> 00:19:47.440
But the bottom line
is that we were

00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:50.560
able to cut our redemption
rate, in a couple years,

00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:53.110
from 27% to 23%.

00:19:53.110 --> 00:19:56.570
So again, that was
tens of millions

00:19:56.570 --> 00:19:59.660
of profit dollars that
dropped to the bottom line.

00:19:59.660 --> 00:20:06.640
And again, we were using R and
RStudio to do these redemption

00:20:06.640 --> 00:20:09.070
control tests.

00:20:09.070 --> 00:20:12.000
So it was just tremendous.

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:12.820
So--

00:20:12.820 --> 00:20:13.690
That's amazing.

00:20:13.690 --> 00:20:17.290
I remember seeing the blog
post where you mentioned that.

00:20:17.290 --> 00:20:19.480
So I want to make sure to
go grab that and put it

00:20:19.480 --> 00:20:23.830
into the chat for
everybody, too.

00:20:23.830 --> 00:20:27.370
But something else I wanted to
ask you is, on the Hangouts,

00:20:27.370 --> 00:20:30.340
we talk a lot about
best practices

00:20:30.340 --> 00:20:33.070
for sharing our work
with executives.

00:20:33.070 --> 00:20:36.280
And it's rare that we actually
have the executive here

00:20:36.280 --> 00:20:36.940
on the call.

00:20:36.940 --> 00:20:39.910
So as a former CEO,
I was wondering

00:20:39.910 --> 00:20:42.970
if you have any
tips for all of us

00:20:42.970 --> 00:20:47.560
on communicating with
executives and sharing our data

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:48.550
work with them.

00:20:48.550 --> 00:20:49.383
ART STEINMETZ: Yeah.

00:20:55.700 --> 00:20:58.080
Two key points.

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:05.870
One is, data visualization
is the most powerful way

00:21:05.870 --> 00:21:09.950
to get a narrative
across quickly.

00:21:09.950 --> 00:21:14.660
So spend time learning good
visualization techniques.

00:21:14.660 --> 00:21:19.790
Even if you're a math
whiz and a coding whiz,

00:21:19.790 --> 00:21:22.280
understand good visualization.

00:21:22.280 --> 00:21:24.080
It's an aesthetic issue.

00:21:24.080 --> 00:21:24.770
It's an art.

00:21:24.770 --> 00:21:27.620
And there's lots of blogs.

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:31.010
There's lots of literature that
talk about good visualization

00:21:31.010 --> 00:21:33.680
and bad visualization.

00:21:33.680 --> 00:21:37.010
And don't use a
linear scale when

00:21:37.010 --> 00:21:41.540
you should be using a log
scale, simple stuff like that,

00:21:41.540 --> 00:21:44.930
because I've seen
so many bad charts.

00:21:44.930 --> 00:21:51.140
And at worst, you're going
to lose your audience

00:21:51.140 --> 00:21:54.230
very quickly.

00:21:54.230 --> 00:21:55.760
Well, not necessarily
even at worst.

00:21:55.760 --> 00:21:57.843
At best, maybe you're going
to lose your audience.

00:21:57.843 --> 00:22:00.530
At worst, your audience will
draw the wrong conclusions

00:22:00.530 --> 00:22:02.450
and make bad decisions.

00:22:02.450 --> 00:22:09.540
Richard Feynman, the physicist,
was on the Challenger disaster

00:22:09.540 --> 00:22:10.040
panel.

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:13.130
That was a space shuttle
that blew up on re-entry.

00:22:13.130 --> 00:22:20.750
And one of the big
takeaways in that inquiry

00:22:20.750 --> 00:22:23.120
was that PowerPoint
was to blame.

00:22:23.120 --> 00:22:30.020
So the other point I
would make is, don't use--

00:22:30.020 --> 00:22:34.790
and I learned this from
reading about the history

00:22:34.790 --> 00:22:36.500
of that inquest--

00:22:36.500 --> 00:22:39.410
is, don't use a
PowerPoint presentation

00:22:39.410 --> 00:22:42.260
when you should be
using a memo that people

00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:46.040
can read in a narrative,
because all of you

00:22:46.040 --> 00:22:49.130
are familiar with the problem
of seeing a PowerPoint slide.

00:22:49.130 --> 00:22:51.740
So you've got it
landscape orientation.

00:22:51.740 --> 00:22:56.540
And you've got this
incredible amount of text on--

00:22:56.540 --> 00:23:02.060
12-point-font text filling up a
slide, as if it's a slide deck.

00:23:02.060 --> 00:23:05.450
And sometimes you're subjected
to sitting in a conference

00:23:05.450 --> 00:23:10.550
room, having that thing with
2 minutes' worth of text

00:23:10.550 --> 00:23:13.160
on a slide that you're
supposed to process.

00:23:13.160 --> 00:23:15.450
Of course, nobody
looks at that at all.

00:23:15.450 --> 00:23:17.840
And they're listening
to, hopefully,

00:23:17.840 --> 00:23:20.180
what the speaker is saying.

00:23:20.180 --> 00:23:24.110
But it's a complete
waste of everyone's time

00:23:24.110 --> 00:23:28.850
to create and display
this giant PowerPoint,

00:23:28.850 --> 00:23:31.290
this massive quantity of
text on a PowerPoint slide.

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:38.600
So don't use PowerPoint when a
pre-read memo would do the job.

00:23:38.600 --> 00:23:42.680
And yeah, some people aren't
going to read the memo.

00:23:42.680 --> 00:23:47.990
But treat your audience with
respect anyway and tell them

00:23:47.990 --> 00:23:52.280
you're going to assume people
have read the materials.

00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:56.600
And then if you're going to have
a PowerPoint or a slide deck,

00:23:56.600 --> 00:24:01.910
have three, four bullet
points, most, on a slide.

00:24:01.910 --> 00:24:04.010
And then have compelling
visualizations

00:24:04.010 --> 00:24:06.110
that tell your story.

00:24:06.110 --> 00:24:10.390
That's going to earn
you far more respect

00:24:10.390 --> 00:24:15.010
than showing that you've
crammed your PowerPoint

00:24:15.010 --> 00:24:18.010
with a million words.

00:24:18.010 --> 00:24:19.240
Thank you.

00:24:19.240 --> 00:24:20.660
That's really helpful.

00:24:20.660 --> 00:24:23.860
I see Art-- or, let's see.

00:24:23.860 --> 00:24:26.140
I think, Alan, you asked
a question a little bit

00:24:26.140 --> 00:24:27.192
earlier in the chat.

00:24:27.192 --> 00:24:28.150
Do you want to jump in?

00:24:30.750 --> 00:24:31.630
Yeah, sure.

00:24:31.630 --> 00:24:33.940
Hi, everybody.

00:24:33.940 --> 00:24:38.820
I'm curious, Art, if in finance
or in portfolio management,

00:24:38.820 --> 00:24:41.790
if you have to be
concerned about things

00:24:41.790 --> 00:24:47.850
like validated systems, software
quality, the sorts of things

00:24:47.850 --> 00:24:50.520
we talk about here a lot
with regards to fina--

00:24:50.520 --> 00:24:53.490
not finance, with the pharma
and med device and stuff.

00:24:53.490 --> 00:24:55.620
Are there places in
the business where

00:24:55.620 --> 00:24:58.230
you've got lots of landscape
to experiment with tools

00:24:58.230 --> 00:25:01.410
and then other places where
there are folks doing work that

00:25:01.410 --> 00:25:05.640
have to be much more diligent
about being validated or being

00:25:05.640 --> 00:25:06.530
controlled?

00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:08.530
And just because I don't
know anything about it,

00:25:08.530 --> 00:25:11.130
are there enterprise
applications

00:25:11.130 --> 00:25:14.340
that are made for purpose for
that specific kind of stuff,

00:25:14.340 --> 00:25:18.120
or does every shop
have their own tools

00:25:18.120 --> 00:25:23.250
that somebody made and
everybody just keeps using them?

00:25:23.250 --> 00:25:25.350
Yeah, good question.

00:25:25.350 --> 00:25:32.770
Like health care, finance is
a highly regulated industry,

00:25:32.770 --> 00:25:35.280
particularly as you're
consumer-facing.

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:38.280
So we have enormous
amounts of regulations

00:25:38.280 --> 00:25:42.120
over what we can say
to people and what

00:25:42.120 --> 00:25:44.910
our responsibilities and
potential liabilities

00:25:44.910 --> 00:25:50.040
are when we misrepresent
what we are saying to people,

00:25:50.040 --> 00:25:51.840
like if you say, you
can't lose money.

00:25:51.840 --> 00:25:54.210
This is a guaranteed win
or something like that,

00:25:54.210 --> 00:25:55.230
to state the obvious.

00:25:55.230 --> 00:26:02.190
But to your point, yes,
there is a huge problem,

00:26:02.190 --> 00:26:05.170
in our industry,
around model risk.

00:26:05.170 --> 00:26:10.440
And if we use any model--

00:26:10.440 --> 00:26:13.560
and that's very liberally
defined, by the way--

00:26:13.560 --> 00:26:15.840
to help us with our
investment decision-making,

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:19.800
and we come back at some point,
or it's disclosed somehow

00:26:19.800 --> 00:26:24.480
that that model had a
coding error, a bug,

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:29.010
and gave an incorrect answer
because of a bug in the code,

00:26:29.010 --> 00:26:33.030
strictly speaking, we are liable
for making investors whole

00:26:33.030 --> 00:26:34.510
for any losses on that.

00:26:34.510 --> 00:26:38.160
And there have been many
instances of lawsuits

00:26:38.160 --> 00:26:41.050
around exactly that.

00:26:41.050 --> 00:26:44.590
So we have to be
very, very careful.

00:26:44.590 --> 00:26:47.460
And as you all know,
there's no such thing

00:26:47.460 --> 00:26:48.630
as bug-free software.

00:26:48.630 --> 00:26:53.490
There will be
errors and problems.

00:26:53.490 --> 00:26:57.150
And it's incumbent on us to
self-report whether or not

00:26:57.150 --> 00:27:00.150
that caused losses.

00:27:00.150 --> 00:27:04.140
And the SEC has leveled
hundreds of millions

00:27:04.140 --> 00:27:08.220
of dollars of fines, over the
last decade, to firms with--

00:27:08.220 --> 00:27:10.650
for model errors.

00:27:10.650 --> 00:27:16.830
And invariably,
whenever people lose

00:27:16.830 --> 00:27:20.730
a lot of money in an investment
that someone else is managing,

00:27:20.730 --> 00:27:23.140
there will be lawsuits.

00:27:23.140 --> 00:27:26.680
So in 2008, there was the
great financial crisis,

00:27:26.680 --> 00:27:29.130
and stocks went down.

00:27:29.130 --> 00:27:30.060
Everything went down.

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:31.227
There were lots of problems.

00:27:31.227 --> 00:27:33.210
And there were lots of lawsuits.

00:27:33.210 --> 00:27:38.010
And the heart of many of
those were, I lost money,

00:27:38.010 --> 00:27:39.090
and I'm mad about it.

00:27:39.090 --> 00:27:41.650
I want somebody
to make me whole.

00:27:41.650 --> 00:27:44.760
So now if I'm
hiring a lawyer, how

00:27:44.760 --> 00:27:50.580
do I craft a case that
says it was somebody else's

00:27:50.580 --> 00:27:53.370
fault, other than
just the market,

00:27:53.370 --> 00:27:56.670
and I therefore
should be made whole?

00:27:56.670 --> 00:27:59.610
One of the first things they
go looking for-- whether or not

00:27:59.610 --> 00:28:02.080
there were models involved
in making the trades,

00:28:02.080 --> 00:28:04.080
whether any robots
were doing the work.

00:28:04.080 --> 00:28:06.390
And then were
there bugs in that?

00:28:06.390 --> 00:28:14.430
So every model is different.

00:28:14.430 --> 00:28:16.150
Every process is different.

00:28:16.150 --> 00:28:21.390
There's no automated
way of saying,

00:28:21.390 --> 00:28:23.310
is this bug-free, obviously.

00:28:23.310 --> 00:28:30.720
But all the big accounting
firms will audit your models

00:28:30.720 --> 00:28:32.260
if you pay them.

00:28:32.260 --> 00:28:33.810
So that's what we had to do.

00:28:33.810 --> 00:28:40.950
And between you and me, if
there was some subtle coding

00:28:40.950 --> 00:28:46.170
error that switched a positive
sign to a negative sign

00:28:46.170 --> 00:28:48.510
somewhere, they're not
going to discover that.

00:28:48.510 --> 00:28:51.970
But nonetheless, that becomes
a legal defense that you say,

00:28:51.970 --> 00:28:57.240
well, I had an auditor
review the code.

00:28:57.240 --> 00:29:03.143
But it is a huge
issue in the industry.

00:29:03.143 --> 00:29:04.310
That's really interesting.

00:29:04.310 --> 00:29:06.200
Thanks.

00:29:06.200 --> 00:29:08.150
I want to make sure
I go over to some

00:29:08.150 --> 00:29:10.550
of the anonymous questions
that were asked, too.

00:29:10.550 --> 00:29:12.440
And I just wanted
to remind everybody,

00:29:12.440 --> 00:29:14.982
because I know I say it in the
beginning, and a lot of people

00:29:14.982 --> 00:29:17.850
join after, if you
want to ask questions,

00:29:17.850 --> 00:29:20.090
feel free to raise
your hand on Zoom.

00:29:20.090 --> 00:29:22.730
Or you can put questions
into the chat here.

00:29:22.730 --> 00:29:25.970
And then we also have the Slido,
where you can ask anonymously.

00:29:25.970 --> 00:29:28.790
And we'll reshare that link
because I know you can't see it

00:29:28.790 --> 00:29:32.090
after you've joined.

00:29:32.090 --> 00:29:34.430
But one of the first
anonymous questions,

00:29:34.430 --> 00:29:37.250
Art, was, how do we
help leadership-- say,

00:29:37.250 --> 00:29:39.380
VP level-- really
understand what

00:29:39.380 --> 00:29:43.070
it is so that they don't just
succumb to the buzzwords,

00:29:43.070 --> 00:29:47.602
like AI, machine learning,
big data, for example?

00:29:47.602 --> 00:29:49.090
Yeah.

00:29:49.090 --> 00:29:51.610
Well, the buzzwords
aren't useless.

00:29:51.610 --> 00:30:00.370
To the extent that a senior
leader in the organization

00:30:00.370 --> 00:30:04.600
gets all excited about
AI and is worried

00:30:04.600 --> 00:30:07.000
about being left
behind, then that

00:30:07.000 --> 00:30:09.560
means they're going to give
you more support for your work.

00:30:09.560 --> 00:30:11.020
They're going to listen to you.

00:30:11.020 --> 00:30:17.110
And then since maybe I'm sitting
in my nice corner office,

00:30:17.110 --> 00:30:18.640
and I'm just hearing
the buzzwords--

00:30:18.640 --> 00:30:23.210
I'm like the boss in Dilbert
or something like that--

00:30:23.210 --> 00:30:26.560
but if I'm willing
to listen, then you

00:30:26.560 --> 00:30:28.840
can give me a
deeper understanding

00:30:28.840 --> 00:30:31.160
of what's involved.

00:30:31.160 --> 00:30:34.210
So I think the buzzwords
are your friend.

00:30:34.210 --> 00:30:38.680
The important thing-- if it
creates any sort of urgency

00:30:38.680 --> 00:30:45.340
or creates more opportunity
for data scientists

00:30:45.340 --> 00:30:48.860
to elevate the
visibility of their work.

00:30:48.860 --> 00:30:51.200
The most important
thing, though,

00:30:51.200 --> 00:30:53.990
is, you have to be honest.

00:30:53.990 --> 00:31:00.490
So when I, as the boss, call
you into my office and say,

00:31:00.490 --> 00:31:02.530
I heard about this
ChatGPT thing.

00:31:02.530 --> 00:31:10.570
Let's fire our entire phone
customer support people

00:31:10.570 --> 00:31:13.600
and just turn it
over to ChatGPT,

00:31:13.600 --> 00:31:19.220
you have to be honest about what
these things can and can't do.

00:31:19.220 --> 00:31:21.880
And one of the things that--

00:31:21.880 --> 00:31:23.260
this will never change.

00:31:23.260 --> 00:31:27.400
This has been true since
the times of Hammurabi--

00:31:27.400 --> 00:31:33.250
that subordinates will
come into the boss' office,

00:31:33.250 --> 00:31:35.740
and they'll blow
sunshine up your skirt.

00:31:35.740 --> 00:31:41.560
And avoiding that,
getting an environment--

00:31:41.560 --> 00:31:43.130
and this is impor--

00:31:43.130 --> 00:31:45.280
this is what the
bosses have to do,

00:31:45.280 --> 00:31:46.690
what the leaders have to do--

00:31:46.690 --> 00:31:51.070
is create an
environment of trust,

00:31:51.070 --> 00:31:56.680
where people can deliver bad
news about what something can

00:31:56.680 --> 00:32:01.330
and cannot accomplish
to the boss.

00:32:01.330 --> 00:32:06.745
And they can't be afraid
of saying, sorry, Boss.

00:32:06.745 --> 00:32:13.230
ChatGPT isn't the
be all and end all.

00:32:13.230 --> 00:32:19.450
And again, I experienced
this firsthand.

00:32:19.450 --> 00:32:22.120
If I would say, oh,
this sounds like fun.

00:32:22.120 --> 00:32:22.620
Let's do it.

00:32:22.620 --> 00:32:24.453
Or this looks like this
will be interesting.

00:32:24.453 --> 00:32:26.550
Let's do it-- people will
start scrambling around

00:32:26.550 --> 00:32:32.220
and try and do it because
the boss likes it.

00:32:32.220 --> 00:32:35.250
And, well, on one
sense, I'll say, good,

00:32:35.250 --> 00:32:36.420
because I'm the boss.

00:32:36.420 --> 00:32:39.270
I'd like to have some
influence over what people do.

00:32:39.270 --> 00:32:45.750
But you have to have
the courage to say, no,

00:32:45.750 --> 00:32:50.860
this is not going to work,
if it's not going to work.

00:32:50.860 --> 00:32:51.820
Thank you.

00:32:51.820 --> 00:32:55.180
I really like that
reframing of, buzzwords

00:32:55.180 --> 00:32:57.607
can be your friend,
as well, when

00:32:57.607 --> 00:33:00.190
you're trying to get your point
across or highlight your work.

00:33:00.190 --> 00:33:01.273
ART STEINMETZ: [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:01.273 --> 00:33:02.590
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Yeah.

00:33:02.590 --> 00:33:05.600
Dave, I see you had asked a
question a bit earlier, too.

00:33:05.600 --> 00:33:07.160
Do you want to jump in next?

00:33:07.160 --> 00:33:07.660
Sure.

00:33:07.660 --> 00:33:09.190
Thanks, Rachael.

00:33:09.190 --> 00:33:09.790
Hi, Art.

00:33:09.790 --> 00:33:13.630
This is Dave Shea from Posit.

00:33:13.630 --> 00:33:16.940
The description you have of
your experience at Oppenheimer

00:33:16.940 --> 00:33:21.190
I know is almost backwards
for a lot of financial firms

00:33:21.190 --> 00:33:24.640
and definitely for
other big organizations.

00:33:24.640 --> 00:33:28.120
This is data-science
excellence and opportunity

00:33:28.120 --> 00:33:31.060
pushed from the top down.

00:33:31.060 --> 00:33:32.860
A couple questions around that.

00:33:32.860 --> 00:33:37.540
Have you had any success in
discussing, with your CEO

00:33:37.540 --> 00:33:40.570
peers, this opportunity?

00:33:40.570 --> 00:33:43.070
And have you won
them over to that?

00:33:43.070 --> 00:33:47.080
And then on the other
side, for folks working up

00:33:47.080 --> 00:33:51.250
the chain, what do you
think is a successful way

00:33:51.250 --> 00:33:56.090
to convey that
opportunity upward?

00:33:56.090 --> 00:33:56.590
Yeah.

00:33:56.590 --> 00:34:01.150
Well, I think, like I said,
it was a different time then.

00:34:01.150 --> 00:34:04.150
But the world has come
around to understanding

00:34:04.150 --> 00:34:05.570
the power of big data.

00:34:05.570 --> 00:34:06.820
Again, it's a buzzword, right?

00:34:06.820 --> 00:34:09.580
Big data, data science.

00:34:09.580 --> 00:34:12.820
And my alma mater,
Denison University,

00:34:12.820 --> 00:34:17.150
has launched a whole
school around data science,

00:34:17.150 --> 00:34:18.100
data analytics.

00:34:18.100 --> 00:34:19.790
And it's a liberal-arts college.

00:34:19.790 --> 00:34:21.385
I was a Russian
major in college.

00:34:23.889 --> 00:34:27.100
But I think CEOs
today very much--

00:34:27.100 --> 00:34:29.050
even if they don't
necessarily understand it,

00:34:29.050 --> 00:34:31.659
going back to the buzzwords,
they think it's important

00:34:31.659 --> 00:34:36.760
and they need to
pay attention to it.

00:34:36.760 --> 00:34:39.550
So I don't see much
resistance to that.

00:34:39.550 --> 00:34:40.480
It's funny, though.

00:34:40.480 --> 00:34:45.750
Like I said, the investment
people in my organization,

00:34:45.750 --> 00:34:48.179
to some extent, still
are very old-school.

00:34:48.179 --> 00:34:51.780
They are very wary of
quantitative techniques

00:34:51.780 --> 00:34:55.150
because they-- there are
many quant funds out there,

00:34:55.150 --> 00:34:59.430
and some of them do great work.

00:34:59.430 --> 00:35:02.200
We are fundamental investors.

00:35:02.200 --> 00:35:05.790
So it's reading annual reports
and talking to management,

00:35:05.790 --> 00:35:08.580
visiting the factories or
whatever it happens to be.

00:35:08.580 --> 00:35:09.930
That's fundamental management.

00:35:09.930 --> 00:35:12.450
We try to beat the market
doing better research,

00:35:12.450 --> 00:35:13.950
in those lines.

00:35:13.950 --> 00:35:15.570
And there was a
lot of resistance

00:35:15.570 --> 00:35:19.320
to quantitative
techniques because--

00:35:19.320 --> 00:35:20.160
we're fundamental.

00:35:20.160 --> 00:35:22.660
We don't want-- we're not going
to let the robots take over.

00:35:22.660 --> 00:35:24.210
Now, the argument I would make--

00:35:24.210 --> 00:35:26.520
I did make-- to--

00:35:26.520 --> 00:35:28.860
when I became chief investment
officer and then CEO,

00:35:28.860 --> 00:35:33.060
the argument I would make
to the investment people--

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:36.160
and I was not going to push
anything down their throats.

00:35:36.160 --> 00:35:40.020
I was trying to evangelize
to make them want to do this.

00:35:40.020 --> 00:35:43.560
But I said to them, look, this
isn't about the robots taking

00:35:43.560 --> 00:35:47.010
your jobs, or we're going to--

00:35:47.010 --> 00:35:49.230
literally, that's what
they were worried about,

00:35:49.230 --> 00:35:51.420
that we were just going
to make these quant funds,

00:35:51.420 --> 00:35:52.290
because we had some.

00:35:52.290 --> 00:35:55.110
We launched-- because
nobody would come around

00:35:55.110 --> 00:35:59.250
on the fundamental side, we
launched some quant funds.

00:35:59.250 --> 00:36:06.300
The argument I made, with
limited success, was,

00:36:06.300 --> 00:36:08.410
look, I'm not talking
about getting rid of you.

00:36:08.410 --> 00:36:11.820
I'm saying, you have been
digging ditches with a shovel

00:36:11.820 --> 00:36:13.020
your whole career.

00:36:13.020 --> 00:36:15.990
Now I come and
give you a backhoe.

00:36:15.990 --> 00:36:18.000
You're not going to use it?

00:36:18.000 --> 00:36:19.560
It's a force multiplier.

00:36:19.560 --> 00:36:22.590
If you can apply these
quantitative tools to suddenly

00:36:22.590 --> 00:36:27.240
process far more data than
you used to be able to,

00:36:27.240 --> 00:36:32.570
it's making your analysts
far more efficient.

00:36:32.570 --> 00:36:36.050
But like I said, in terms
of the rest-- other CEOs,

00:36:36.050 --> 00:36:38.270
other people in
asset management,

00:36:38.270 --> 00:36:40.520
everyone has come
around, I think, to that.

00:36:40.520 --> 00:36:46.250
And so I would be interested in
hearing what other people say

00:36:46.250 --> 00:36:48.500
about this, because
you folks are

00:36:48.500 --> 00:36:52.070
more mid-career, early
career than I certainly am,

00:36:52.070 --> 00:36:52.947
as retired.

00:36:52.947 --> 00:36:54.530
You would probably
have a better sense

00:36:54.530 --> 00:36:58.640
of the receptivity in your
organization to using more

00:36:58.640 --> 00:37:00.425
powerful quantitative tools.

00:37:03.850 --> 00:37:06.640
I noticed a few people
resonated with when you

00:37:06.640 --> 00:37:08.590
said living on your own island.

00:37:08.590 --> 00:37:13.210
So [CHUCKLES] a lot of people
were feeling that, too.

00:37:13.210 --> 00:37:16.240
What I hope-- and again,
this worked to some extent

00:37:16.240 --> 00:37:18.760
in my organization.

00:37:18.760 --> 00:37:21.390
And it's happening right now
at Invesco, too, I should add.

00:37:21.390 --> 00:37:24.450
People look over your shoulder
and say, wow, that is cool.

00:37:24.450 --> 00:37:25.770
How do you do that?

00:37:25.770 --> 00:37:30.190
Or, I see a way I could apply
what you're doing there.

00:37:30.190 --> 00:37:32.850
So for instance, at
Invesco right now--

00:37:32.850 --> 00:37:37.080
and this started
after I had left--

00:37:37.080 --> 00:37:39.450
one of their folks,
again in sales,

00:37:39.450 --> 00:37:48.310
has created a Shiny app that
if one of our customers,

00:37:48.310 --> 00:37:51.640
which is to say our brokers,
shares their customers,

00:37:51.640 --> 00:37:55.920
which is the end investor,
their portfolios with us--

00:37:55.920 --> 00:37:58.320
what other funds they're
invested in, what other stocks

00:37:58.320 --> 00:38:02.820
they're invested in, whatever--
we can run an analysis on that

00:38:02.820 --> 00:38:07.830
to help them optimize
their tax situation--

00:38:07.830 --> 00:38:09.870
when they should
take capital gains,

00:38:09.870 --> 00:38:11.850
when they should
take capital losses,

00:38:11.850 --> 00:38:15.400
what they should sell to
realize a loss or a gain,

00:38:15.400 --> 00:38:18.570
and then if they want to
keep the portfolio complexion

00:38:18.570 --> 00:38:24.150
the same, what should they then
buy on the other side to keep

00:38:24.150 --> 00:38:26.250
the portfolio complexion--

00:38:26.250 --> 00:38:29.758
the overall risk
or aggressiveness,

00:38:29.758 --> 00:38:31.800
whatever it happens to
be-- the overall portfolio

00:38:31.800 --> 00:38:33.160
complexion the same.

00:38:33.160 --> 00:38:35.770
So he built a Shiny
app that does that.

00:38:35.770 --> 00:38:39.060
So all of our salespeople,
all of Invesco salespeople,

00:38:39.060 --> 00:38:45.430
can look at all of the advisors
that they serve and look

00:38:45.430 --> 00:38:45.930
at that.

00:38:45.930 --> 00:38:48.240
And that generates discussions.

00:38:48.240 --> 00:38:51.990
And salespeople love
to have opportunities

00:38:51.990 --> 00:38:55.890
to call their customers up and
have an interesting discussion

00:38:55.890 --> 00:38:57.960
and say, look, here's
an issue you have

00:38:57.960 --> 00:39:00.165
that I think I can
help you solve.

00:39:00.165 --> 00:39:02.040
Salespeople love getting
that kind of a lead.

00:39:02.040 --> 00:39:03.790
So it generates
that sort of thing.

00:39:03.790 --> 00:39:07.050
And then if the discussion
goes further, with one click,

00:39:07.050 --> 00:39:10.740
they generate a
detailed PDF report

00:39:10.740 --> 00:39:17.490
on exactly what the
gains and losses are

00:39:17.490 --> 00:39:18.660
across the portfolio.

00:39:18.660 --> 00:39:21.810
And the salespeople
just love that tool.

00:39:21.810 --> 00:39:24.240
But it's one individual.

00:39:24.240 --> 00:39:27.750
And my fear is that
it dies with him.

00:39:27.750 --> 00:39:30.990
If he leaves the firm, who's
going to maintain that?

00:39:30.990 --> 00:39:32.340
It's an island.

00:39:32.340 --> 00:39:36.930
And yet, it's a Shiny
app in production

00:39:36.930 --> 00:39:42.730
that has become an
extremely popular tool

00:39:42.730 --> 00:39:45.040
among 200 salespeople.

00:39:45.040 --> 00:39:51.610
So it's an interesting
opportunity.

00:39:51.610 --> 00:39:58.180
And this person is not naturally
a real outgoing, gregarious

00:39:58.180 --> 00:40:00.010
individual.

00:40:00.010 --> 00:40:04.840
So I've been trying to encourage
him to think about ways that he

00:40:04.840 --> 00:40:09.520
can let other people know,
this is a tool that he built,

00:40:09.520 --> 00:40:15.340
and you can have this kind of a
cool tool, also, in your area.

00:40:15.340 --> 00:40:17.980
So getting him off
the island is--

00:40:17.980 --> 00:40:20.956
I've made it a--

00:40:20.956 --> 00:40:22.690
I have no vested
interest in this.

00:40:22.690 --> 00:40:25.510
I've kind of made it my mission.

00:40:25.510 --> 00:40:26.950
Absolutely.

00:40:26.950 --> 00:40:31.000
It's really interesting to
think about, maybe some of us

00:40:31.000 --> 00:40:33.880
could be targeting our work
to different groups of people

00:40:33.880 --> 00:40:36.370
within the company, like
sales, which we may not

00:40:36.370 --> 00:40:37.900
have thought of before.

00:40:37.900 --> 00:40:40.540
And I see somebody had
asked an anonymous question.

00:40:40.540 --> 00:40:43.900
That sales was so
interested in the data

00:40:43.900 --> 00:40:45.400
was really interesting to them.

00:40:45.400 --> 00:40:47.560
And they asked, were
there any groups

00:40:47.560 --> 00:40:49.780
that you were surprised
weren't interested,

00:40:49.780 --> 00:40:51.980
and why do you think that was?

00:40:51.980 --> 00:40:52.480
Yeah.

00:40:52.480 --> 00:40:54.430
Well, again, I've already told
the story about the investment

00:40:54.430 --> 00:40:54.850
people.

00:40:54.850 --> 00:40:55.767
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Yeah.

00:40:55.767 --> 00:40:58.150
ART STEINMETZ: And they
were pretty skeptical.

00:40:58.150 --> 00:41:00.610
The other group, as well--

00:41:00.610 --> 00:41:04.690
is it worth the cost?

00:41:04.690 --> 00:41:07.020
What's the IRR on
this investment?

00:41:07.020 --> 00:41:12.610
And again, in the anecdote
I shared about redemption

00:41:12.610 --> 00:41:15.520
control, the IRR was
strongly positive,

00:41:15.520 --> 00:41:17.930
but it's not always
going to be that way.

00:41:17.930 --> 00:41:21.400
But I think it is important to
have a clear-eyed understanding

00:41:21.400 --> 00:41:27.820
of what the IRR is going to
be on a project like this.

00:41:27.820 --> 00:41:29.380
What I would not
be concerned about

00:41:29.380 --> 00:41:31.000
is whether there
are enough projects

00:41:31.000 --> 00:41:32.170
for a data-science team.

00:41:32.170 --> 00:41:39.780
In most organizations, you
might have a particular use case

00:41:39.780 --> 00:41:43.410
when you spin up some kind of
a data-science organization.

00:41:43.410 --> 00:41:45.750
But like I said, very
quickly, other people

00:41:45.750 --> 00:41:51.090
will see the cool stuff that
is happening and want in on it.

00:41:55.440 --> 00:42:01.030
Oh, one of the questions I saw,
going back into the chat, was,

00:42:01.030 --> 00:42:05.563
what books you might
recommend that help you

00:42:05.563 --> 00:42:07.480
throughout your career
to develop your mindset

00:42:07.480 --> 00:42:09.280
as a data-science CEO.

00:42:09.280 --> 00:42:13.070
Well, let's see here.

00:42:13.070 --> 00:42:16.450
I already talked
about Tufte, right?

00:42:16.450 --> 00:42:20.580
So he's got a whole
bunch of books.

00:42:20.580 --> 00:42:27.840
But this is the one that
started it all, The Visual Dis--

00:42:27.840 --> 00:42:28.490
[INAUDIBLE]--

00:42:28.490 --> 00:42:29.280
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: I can see it.

00:42:29.280 --> 00:42:29.616
ART STEINMETZ:
--The Visual Display

00:42:29.616 --> 00:42:31.530
of Quantitative Information.

00:42:31.530 --> 00:42:34.710
And this guy is kind of
a lovable curmudgeon,

00:42:34.710 --> 00:42:35.820
kind of a crank.

00:42:35.820 --> 00:42:39.000
But he's passionate
about making sure

00:42:39.000 --> 00:42:42.420
that your visualizations
convey the information that you

00:42:42.420 --> 00:42:43.840
want them to convey.

00:42:43.840 --> 00:42:46.650
So his whole series
of books-- they're

00:42:46.650 --> 00:42:53.170
all classics in the field.

00:42:53.170 --> 00:42:56.580
Another one to pull off
the shelf that I think

00:42:56.580 --> 00:43:00.840
is a ripping yarn
is Against the Gods

00:43:00.840 --> 00:43:03.450
by Peter Bernstein, The
Remarkable Story of Risk.

00:43:03.450 --> 00:43:05.670
For anyone who
wants to understand

00:43:05.670 --> 00:43:11.550
how we went from thinking
everything is just

00:43:11.550 --> 00:43:13.920
in the hands of the
gods, and it's all fate,

00:43:13.920 --> 00:43:20.370
to being able to price
derivatives using

00:43:20.370 --> 00:43:23.160
normal distributions and
understanding probability,

00:43:23.160 --> 00:43:24.090
this is a great book.

00:43:24.090 --> 00:43:26.290
And this is not
dry in the least.

00:43:26.290 --> 00:43:29.220
But I think it's very
useful for understanding how

00:43:29.220 --> 00:43:31.140
the history of risk evolved.

00:43:31.140 --> 00:43:35.250
The ancient Romans were
inveterate gamblers,

00:43:35.250 --> 00:43:37.450
but they had no
sense of probability.

00:43:37.450 --> 00:43:40.530
So if you wanted
to go play cards,

00:43:40.530 --> 00:43:43.920
play poker with the Romans,
you could really fleece them.

00:43:43.920 --> 00:43:49.440
But it was Blaise
Pascal, who was

00:43:49.440 --> 00:43:53.070
a philosopher and
mathematician, who

00:43:53.070 --> 00:43:55.050
really developed probability.

00:43:55.050 --> 00:43:58.050
And he did it because
he was a gambler.

00:44:00.780 --> 00:44:07.672
So there's another book
that got me thinking about--

00:44:07.672 --> 00:44:09.130
it's out of reach
because it's over

00:44:09.130 --> 00:44:12.550
in my business-books section.

00:44:12.550 --> 00:44:19.360
But there's another book called
The Outsiders by Richard--

00:44:19.360 --> 00:44:22.540
his last name is Thorndike.

00:44:22.540 --> 00:44:24.190
I'm not sure his
first name is Richard.

00:44:24.190 --> 00:44:27.250
But he has a great
book for thinking

00:44:27.250 --> 00:44:33.520
about how CEOs should
think about maximizing

00:44:33.520 --> 00:44:36.250
the value of their businesses.

00:44:36.250 --> 00:44:39.040
And that had a profound
influence on me.

00:44:39.040 --> 00:44:42.190
One of the things that is true--

00:44:42.190 --> 00:44:46.130
if you look at most mergers
of publicly traded companies,

00:44:46.130 --> 00:44:51.370
the vast majority,
3 plus 3 equals

00:44:51.370 --> 00:44:56.150
5, which is to say that
the mergers are usually

00:44:56.150 --> 00:44:58.770
destructive of
shareholder value.

00:44:58.770 --> 00:45:04.610
And I won't name names,
but I've experienced

00:45:04.610 --> 00:45:08.120
this so often in my career.

00:45:08.120 --> 00:45:12.920
And the problem is, the
CEO of the combined entity

00:45:12.920 --> 00:45:16.400
now gets to say, I
grew the company.

00:45:16.400 --> 00:45:17.720
It's much bigger now.

00:45:17.720 --> 00:45:20.870
And he, of course-- he or she--

00:45:20.870 --> 00:45:23.180
obviously, usually a white man--

00:45:23.180 --> 00:45:27.110
is running a much
bigger organization.

00:45:27.110 --> 00:45:32.120
And that could be used to
justify the board paying him

00:45:32.120 --> 00:45:34.460
a lot more generous
compensation.

00:45:34.460 --> 00:45:39.170
And he gets the
reputation as a dealmaker.

00:45:39.170 --> 00:45:44.480
But the problem is, if the two
companies were worth 6 apart,

00:45:44.480 --> 00:45:48.390
and they're now worth
5, you lost money.

00:45:48.390 --> 00:45:49.830
You didn't do a good job.

00:45:49.830 --> 00:45:55.590
This book, The Outsiders, really
cracks that open and does a lot

00:45:55.590 --> 00:45:57.480
of-- as business
books like to do,

00:45:57.480 --> 00:46:00.540
there's a lot of anecdotes
and case studies--

00:46:00.540 --> 00:46:05.880
shows a few CEOs who understood
that and bucked that trend

00:46:05.880 --> 00:46:09.630
and really thought
about how to grow

00:46:09.630 --> 00:46:14.310
the wealth of their
shareholders, which

00:46:14.310 --> 00:46:17.730
is what they're accountable to.

00:46:17.730 --> 00:46:22.610
One of the things that--

00:46:22.610 --> 00:46:24.950
whenever companies
get mashed together,

00:46:24.950 --> 00:46:27.110
there's talk of
cost savings, right?

00:46:27.110 --> 00:46:29.900
You have synergies, economies
of scale, et cetera.

00:46:29.900 --> 00:46:31.320
And that's all well and good.

00:46:31.320 --> 00:46:36.860
But there's hardly ever any
talk about revenue synergies,

00:46:36.860 --> 00:46:39.080
because there
seldom are any, even

00:46:39.080 --> 00:46:44.060
though there will be some
genuflecting in that direction.

00:46:44.060 --> 00:46:48.710
So anyway, that
book, I thought, was

00:46:48.710 --> 00:46:51.373
very formative in my
thinking as someone

00:46:51.373 --> 00:46:52.790
who was running a
company, someone

00:46:52.790 --> 00:46:56.270
who bought other companies,
and who ultimately sold

00:46:56.270 --> 00:46:59.880
my company to another company.

00:46:59.880 --> 00:47:02.100
I love this-- just grabbing
the books off the back.

00:47:02.100 --> 00:47:04.380
It's like book
club with Art here.

00:47:04.380 --> 00:47:05.530
[LAUGHTER]

00:47:05.530 --> 00:47:07.080
This is--

00:47:07.080 --> 00:47:12.580
I'm a book-- I have a fetish
b I fetishize them as objects.

00:47:12.580 --> 00:47:16.440
And so I really like
sitting in my library

00:47:16.440 --> 00:47:20.150
with a Manhattan in the
evening and reading a book.

00:47:20.150 --> 00:47:21.330
Nice.

00:47:21.330 --> 00:47:25.580
So I know something that
you are also really involved

00:47:25.580 --> 00:47:33.260
with is enhancing data literacy
and mathematical literacy.

00:47:33.260 --> 00:47:35.840
And you've been involved
with Rock The Street,

00:47:35.840 --> 00:47:38.600
Wall Street, whose mission is
to equip girls with the skills

00:47:38.600 --> 00:47:42.540
to succeed, and many
other organizations.

00:47:42.540 --> 00:47:44.150
And I wanted to give
some time to talk

00:47:44.150 --> 00:47:46.150
a little bit about that,
too, and see if there's

00:47:46.150 --> 00:47:48.680
any that you'd recommend
we get involved in

00:47:48.680 --> 00:47:51.570
or ways to get more involved.

00:47:51.570 --> 00:47:55.610
Yeah, thanks for
asking that, because it's

00:47:55.610 --> 00:47:56.840
obviously a worthwhile topic.

00:47:56.840 --> 00:47:58.430
I don't have to
sell anybody here

00:47:58.430 --> 00:48:03.170
on the value of being
data-literate or understanding

00:48:03.170 --> 00:48:05.600
basic math.

00:48:05.600 --> 00:48:12.182
But there's a weird cultural
problem I think we have,

00:48:12.182 --> 00:48:13.640
certainly in this
country and maybe

00:48:13.640 --> 00:48:18.260
in a lot of other countries-- is
that it's OK to be bad at math.

00:48:18.260 --> 00:48:21.530
People are perfectly
happy, maybe

00:48:21.530 --> 00:48:24.350
even proud, to say,
oh, I'm bad at math.

00:48:24.350 --> 00:48:28.190
It's like-- no one says,
oh, I'm bad at reading.

00:48:28.190 --> 00:48:31.490
Oh, I'm proud to be illiterate.

00:48:31.490 --> 00:48:35.630
Numeracy should be
something that we all

00:48:35.630 --> 00:48:37.850
are very proud of having.

00:48:37.850 --> 00:48:43.280
And the consequences of not
being numerate are horrible.

00:48:43.280 --> 00:48:45.350
And you see, in--

00:48:45.350 --> 00:48:49.400
our country has become so
polarized, politically.

00:48:49.400 --> 00:48:52.500
And the rhetoric that
goes back and forth--

00:48:52.500 --> 00:48:58.490
so much of the
persuasive power of that

00:48:58.490 --> 00:49:03.470
is grounded on people not
understanding numbers, I find,

00:49:03.470 --> 00:49:05.720
and not being willing to--

00:49:05.720 --> 00:49:07.760
and being very
skeptical of science,

00:49:07.760 --> 00:49:11.930
being very skeptical
of experts, like those

00:49:11.930 --> 00:49:15.740
are elites or something
like that, when all of you

00:49:15.740 --> 00:49:19.350
probably went to college,
and you learn a lot of stuff,

00:49:19.350 --> 00:49:22.070
and you went on to graduate
school or in your careers,

00:49:22.070 --> 00:49:25.430
and you're very proud of being
an expert in your field and all

00:49:25.430 --> 00:49:26.570
the knowledge you created.

00:49:26.570 --> 00:49:29.630
And the idea that that just
should be devalued somehow

00:49:29.630 --> 00:49:33.230
because people need to feel
good about being ignorant

00:49:33.230 --> 00:49:36.500
is an unfortunate
state of affairs.

00:49:36.500 --> 00:49:39.410
I wish we could
get back to a point

00:49:39.410 --> 00:49:42.680
where knowledge is
respected in whatever field

00:49:42.680 --> 00:49:43.890
that happens to be.

00:49:43.890 --> 00:49:47.690
But certainly, one of the ways
to do that and make all of us

00:49:47.690 --> 00:49:51.380
more successful in our
own lives is to make sure

00:49:51.380 --> 00:49:58.313
that kids understand--

00:49:58.313 --> 00:50:01.850
know that it's-- cool
is too strong a word,

00:50:01.850 --> 00:50:07.500
but cool to know math and be
proud of having math skills.

00:50:07.500 --> 00:50:13.860
And so I was involved with the
National Museum of Mathematics,

00:50:13.860 --> 00:50:14.450
which--

00:50:14.450 --> 00:50:16.520
their mission is
to make math cool.

00:50:16.520 --> 00:50:18.290
And it's not a kids'
museum, by the way.

00:50:18.290 --> 00:50:22.050
They have tons of kids
programs, and probably

00:50:22.050 --> 00:50:24.050
most of the visitors to
the museum are children.

00:50:24.050 --> 00:50:30.770
But it really shares the
wonder of mathematical concepts

00:50:30.770 --> 00:50:32.790
at an adult level as well.

00:50:32.790 --> 00:50:36.200
So if you're ever in Manhattan,
it's absolutely worth a visit,

00:50:36.200 --> 00:50:40.400
because it's a very cool
exploratorium kind of museum.

00:50:40.400 --> 00:50:45.860
It's just not definite integrals
printed on a poster on the wall

00:50:45.860 --> 00:50:47.210
or something.

00:50:47.210 --> 00:50:48.620
It's a very cool museum.

00:50:48.620 --> 00:50:58.390
And one of the problems,
obviously, in poor communities,

00:50:58.390 --> 00:51:00.550
underprivileged communities,
whatever word you

00:51:00.550 --> 00:51:03.850
would like to describe them--

00:51:03.850 --> 00:51:08.380
minority communities-- the
schools are generally poor.

00:51:08.380 --> 00:51:15.580
And Rock The Street, Wall Street
brings a finance curriculum

00:51:15.580 --> 00:51:23.410
into underserved schools,
using volunteers.

00:51:23.410 --> 00:51:27.040
So the business model, if you
will, of Rock The Street is,

00:51:27.040 --> 00:51:31.600
we get a corporate sponsor
and a corporate volunteer

00:51:31.600 --> 00:51:32.890
from that sponsor.

00:51:32.890 --> 00:51:36.220
And they teach a--

00:51:36.220 --> 00:51:39.080
call it a finance
course, to kids.

00:51:39.080 --> 00:51:43.330
And one of the purposes of
that is to get more girls--

00:51:43.330 --> 00:51:47.230
because it is focused on
girls-- and I use the word girls

00:51:47.230 --> 00:51:49.540
because this is high school--

00:51:49.540 --> 00:51:53.200
get girls interested in
careers in financial services.

00:51:53.200 --> 00:51:57.550
And while we know less
than half of the girls

00:51:57.550 --> 00:51:59.620
actually go into
financial services--

00:51:59.620 --> 00:52:01.540
but every single
one of them is going

00:52:01.540 --> 00:52:04.450
to get out of this program
with basic financial literacy.

00:52:04.450 --> 00:52:07.930
They are going to
understand that the credit

00:52:07.930 --> 00:52:11.620
limit on your Mastercard
isn't an asset,

00:52:11.620 --> 00:52:17.020
and paying 25% a year
in compound interest

00:52:17.020 --> 00:52:21.680
on your credit-card balance is a
really bad problem to get into.

00:52:21.680 --> 00:52:29.310
So those kind of basic issues
around financial life skills

00:52:29.310 --> 00:52:30.860
is an important part
of what they do.

00:52:30.860 --> 00:52:33.765
And of course, they go into
these less-served communities.

00:52:33.765 --> 00:52:35.140
And one of the
things that just--

00:52:35.140 --> 00:52:40.555
it breaks my heart, and why
I'm passionate about this-- is,

00:52:40.555 --> 00:52:43.660
you've all heard this term
"the cycle of poverty."

00:52:43.660 --> 00:52:46.660
And this cycle of ignorance
exists in these communities

00:52:46.660 --> 00:52:47.260
as well.

00:52:47.260 --> 00:52:52.060
And in many cases, obviously,
the family situation

00:52:52.060 --> 00:52:55.690
is not a traditional
two-parent family.

00:52:55.690 --> 00:52:58.180
And even when it
is, you have issues

00:52:58.180 --> 00:53:00.940
where there isn't
respect for learning

00:53:00.940 --> 00:53:02.290
in some of these families.

00:53:02.290 --> 00:53:04.030
They say, why do you--

00:53:04.030 --> 00:53:05.370
that's not for you.

00:53:05.370 --> 00:53:06.970
You don't have a chance.

00:53:06.970 --> 00:53:10.690
The system is
rigged against you.

00:53:10.690 --> 00:53:11.960
Why bother?

00:53:11.960 --> 00:53:16.120
And fighting against
familial culture,

00:53:16.120 --> 00:53:20.950
in some cases, to give these
girls the belief that, yes, you

00:53:20.950 --> 00:53:23.140
can achieve--

00:53:23.140 --> 00:53:26.920
that doesn't mean the road is
easy, but this is possible.

00:53:26.920 --> 00:53:29.920
And it just takes hard
work and learning,

00:53:29.920 --> 00:53:32.680
and you can achieve that.

00:53:32.680 --> 00:53:36.550
So Rock The Street does
great work swimming

00:53:36.550 --> 00:53:38.990
against that tide of ignorance.

00:53:38.990 --> 00:53:44.230
So it's been heartbreaking
to see some of this stuff,

00:53:44.230 --> 00:53:47.470
but also so inspiring
to see the change

00:53:47.470 --> 00:53:49.480
that it can make in
some kids' lives.

00:53:49.480 --> 00:53:50.647
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Absolutely.

00:53:50.647 --> 00:53:51.880
So helping other--

00:53:51.880 --> 00:53:58.090
whatever it happens
to be in helping kids

00:53:58.090 --> 00:54:02.230
understand early on
that they can achieve,

00:54:02.230 --> 00:54:05.980
that learning and knowledge
are valuable things

00:54:05.980 --> 00:54:09.190
to have-- it seems crazy
to have to say that.

00:54:09.190 --> 00:54:12.790
But girls, in particular,
are underrepresented--

00:54:12.790 --> 00:54:15.430
or, women, in particular, are
very underrepresented in asset

00:54:15.430 --> 00:54:16.100
management.

00:54:16.100 --> 00:54:17.517
So that was one
of the things that

00:54:17.517 --> 00:54:21.700
got me interested in Rock
The Street to begin with.

00:54:21.700 --> 00:54:26.930
We need lots more representation
among people of color as well.

00:54:26.930 --> 00:54:30.740
But women also are, again,
very underrepresented.

00:54:30.740 --> 00:54:32.560
So that's just trying
to get a little more

00:54:32.560 --> 00:54:34.780
inclusivity in finance.

00:54:34.780 --> 00:54:38.260
But also, what Rock The Street,
in particular, recognize

00:54:38.260 --> 00:54:43.570
is that girls self-select out
of STEM fields very early.

00:54:43.570 --> 00:54:49.420
The cultural background is such
that women are told, explicitly

00:54:49.420 --> 00:54:51.880
or implicitly-- girls
are told explicitly

00:54:51.880 --> 00:54:54.730
or implicitly-- that
they're not good at math,

00:54:54.730 --> 00:54:57.710
they're not good at science,
or that's not for them.

00:54:57.710 --> 00:55:00.460
And again, it doesn't
have to be explicit.

00:55:00.460 --> 00:55:03.130
But we find that they
self-select out of STEM

00:55:03.130 --> 00:55:05.410
very early, in high school.

00:55:05.410 --> 00:55:08.500
And that's why it's important
to-- there's lots of groups

00:55:08.500 --> 00:55:12.040
that help give women
early career support.

00:55:12.040 --> 00:55:16.330
Or, in college, there's
Girls Who Code, for instance.

00:55:16.330 --> 00:55:17.675
That's more college level.

00:55:17.675 --> 00:55:19.300
Getting down into
the high-school level

00:55:19.300 --> 00:55:21.735
is critical because
of this problem.

00:55:21.735 --> 00:55:22.600
Absolutely.

00:55:22.600 --> 00:55:24.260
Thank you so much
for sharing that.

00:55:24.260 --> 00:55:26.560
And I did put into the chat,
if there are other groups

00:55:26.560 --> 00:55:28.360
that people are
involved with as well

00:55:28.360 --> 00:55:31.360
and want to share in
the chat, please do.

00:55:31.360 --> 00:55:34.000
I know that our time
goes by so quickly here.

00:55:34.000 --> 00:55:37.720
Is it OK if I rapid-fire a
few questions at you, Art?

00:55:37.720 --> 00:55:40.420
Thank you for listening
to me blab, folks.

00:55:40.420 --> 00:55:42.160
We love it.

00:55:42.160 --> 00:55:44.342
So one anonymous
question maybe touches

00:55:44.342 --> 00:55:45.550
on things we've talked about.

00:55:45.550 --> 00:55:49.840
But can you say, what's the
number-one reason you think,

00:55:49.840 --> 00:55:52.510
whether it's financial
services or any company--

00:55:52.510 --> 00:55:57.840
should move to open-source
languages, like R and Python?

00:55:57.840 --> 00:56:02.190
Yeah, because
of the ecosystem.

00:56:02.190 --> 00:56:04.320
With R or Python,
it's the packages

00:56:04.320 --> 00:56:06.360
that will do anything you want.

00:56:06.360 --> 00:56:09.930
And these packages, of course,
are constantly peer-reviewed.

00:56:13.255 --> 00:56:17.240
There was a notion,
10, 15 years ago,

00:56:17.240 --> 00:56:20.480
that commercial software was
better because you had people

00:56:20.480 --> 00:56:24.260
who were paid to mind
it, whereas open-source,

00:56:24.260 --> 00:56:25.400
anybody could do anything.

00:56:25.400 --> 00:56:28.160
And who knows whether
the code is any good?

00:56:28.160 --> 00:56:31.010
Which-- the truth was the
opposite of that, right?

00:56:31.010 --> 00:56:33.230
When you've got a million
eyes on something,

00:56:33.230 --> 00:56:36.560
errors are spotted
much more quickly.

00:56:36.560 --> 00:56:38.420
Now, there were
commercial software firms

00:56:38.420 --> 00:56:43.340
that had a vested interest
in perpetuating that notion.

00:56:43.340 --> 00:56:45.470
[LAUGHS] There was a blog post.

00:56:45.470 --> 00:56:46.820
It was taken down--

00:56:46.820 --> 00:56:49.280
I guess it was SAS--

00:56:49.280 --> 00:56:52.640
where they were ripping
open-source code.

00:56:52.640 --> 00:57:01.850
And they made a very kind of
disgusting dig that could--

00:57:01.850 --> 00:57:03.440
at Hadley Wickham.

00:57:03.440 --> 00:57:07.610
They said, do you want
trained professionals auditing

00:57:07.610 --> 00:57:11.482
your code, or do you want
some drunk guy in New Zealand?

00:57:11.482 --> 00:57:16.565
[LAUGHS] Which I thought
could only mean Hadley.

00:57:16.565 --> 00:57:17.690
Hadley is from New Zealand.

00:57:17.690 --> 00:57:19.940
I have no idea whether
he enjoys liquor at all.

00:57:19.940 --> 00:57:20.600
But why would they say that?

00:57:20.600 --> 00:57:21.642
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: [LAUGHS]

00:57:21.642 --> 00:57:23.580
It's ridiculous.

00:57:23.580 --> 00:57:27.200
But it just shows the
nastiness that was out there.

00:57:27.200 --> 00:57:34.250
But it's clear that the
huge ecosystem of packages

00:57:34.250 --> 00:57:37.070
maintained by
passionate individuals

00:57:37.070 --> 00:57:40.460
acting as a community is what
gives open-source its power.

00:57:43.080 --> 00:57:45.000
I never saw that
blog post, but now I'm

00:57:45.000 --> 00:57:45.917
interested to go and--

00:57:45.917 --> 00:57:46.830
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:57:46.830 --> 00:57:47.712
[LAUGHS]

00:57:47.712 --> 00:57:49.920
--because they didn't
leave it up for very long.

00:57:49.920 --> 00:57:53.610
One other anonymous question
was, as a data person,

00:57:53.610 --> 00:57:57.360
what are the best ways to
develop domain and business

00:57:57.360 --> 00:57:58.200
knowledge?

00:57:58.200 --> 00:58:01.200
They said, it benefits me to
talk with the business people,

00:58:01.200 --> 00:58:04.500
but not systematically.

00:58:04.500 --> 00:58:09.600
Yeah, that's kind
of a tough problem

00:58:09.600 --> 00:58:13.980
because it requires you being
an advocate for yourself

00:58:13.980 --> 00:58:14.970
in the organization.

00:58:14.970 --> 00:58:16.860
And not all of us are
equipped to do that.

00:58:16.860 --> 00:58:18.240
I wasn't.

00:58:18.240 --> 00:58:21.210
My path to the top
of the organization

00:58:21.210 --> 00:58:22.920
was very serendipitous.

00:58:22.920 --> 00:58:25.480
And I'll spare you the
details on that as well.

00:58:25.480 --> 00:58:29.220
But you need to be able to
get out there and advocate

00:58:29.220 --> 00:58:30.070
for yourself.

00:58:30.070 --> 00:58:36.120
So you have to show up and stick
your nose in when there are

00:58:36.120 --> 00:58:41.310
discussions about the business.

00:58:41.310 --> 00:58:47.200
And hopefully, you
can present yourself

00:58:47.200 --> 00:58:54.360
as someone who is eager
to help solve problems.

00:58:54.360 --> 00:58:56.800
If you say, look,
I'm here because I

00:58:56.800 --> 00:59:00.550
want to understand the problem
so I can do better helping you,

00:59:00.550 --> 00:59:02.740
however you want
to say that, that's

00:59:02.740 --> 00:59:06.100
the kind of approach that
always worked with me when

00:59:06.100 --> 00:59:07.330
people say--

00:59:07.330 --> 00:59:12.020
because I certainly could have
no objection to someone who's,

00:59:12.020 --> 00:59:14.470
say, in IT coming
around saying, I

00:59:14.470 --> 00:59:17.530
want to understand
how you run money

00:59:17.530 --> 00:59:20.830
better so I can understand
your needs better.

00:59:20.830 --> 00:59:26.300
No one is going to say,
oh, get out of my face.

00:59:26.300 --> 00:59:30.980
But it does require you getting
out of your comfort zone,

00:59:30.980 --> 00:59:34.750
perhaps, and being an
advocate for yourself.

00:59:34.750 --> 00:59:35.920
Definitely.

00:59:35.920 --> 00:59:39.410
I just want to make sure I had
seen everybody's questions.

00:59:39.410 --> 00:59:41.635
So feel free to
raise your hand here.

00:59:41.635 --> 00:59:44.780
And Art, if you have to
run at the top of the hour,

00:59:44.780 --> 00:59:45.750
just let us know.

00:59:45.750 --> 00:59:46.290
I just want to give
people a last second.

00:59:46.290 --> 00:59:47.227
[INAUDIBLE]

